“Liam, she needs—” “No.” The word came out hard, sharp. Immediate.
A pause. “Liam—”
“If we call an ambulance, she loses everything,” I cut in, voice flat and final, even as something frantic clawed at my ribs. “You get that, right?”

Silence.

Chloe swore under her breath. Alex exhaled, ragged. “Her scholarship,” Chloe muttered. “Fuck.”
“If she’s taken to a hospital, they’ll put it in her record,” I continued, every word coming out too smooth, too certain, because if I let an inch of panic through, I’d fucking lose it. “They’ll send her to rehab. Or worse—home.”

That landed. I could practically hear them looking at each other, realizing the weight of it.

A suspected overdose wouldn’t just be a wake-up call. It would be the end of her life as she knew it.

I couldn’t let that happen. I wouldn’t.

I pressed down on the gas, the sport houses and dorms blurring past me.

“Keep her breathing,” I ordered, voice sharp. “Shake her. Keep her awake as much as you can. I’ll be there in less than five.”

Chloe let out a shaky exhale. “Hurry.” The line went dead. I didn’t slow down.
I should have seen this coming. I should have stepped in before she did this to herself. But I didn’t. I had to fix it.

The moment I reached Caroline’s dorm, I didn’t knock. I shoved the door open, and the second I stepped inside, I knew it was bad.

The room reeked of wine. Chloe was kneeling by the bed, shaking Caroline’s shoulders. Hard. Alex stood near the desk, face pale, one hand gripping the back of a chair like she needed to hold onto something solid.

And Caroline—

She was still, too fucking still. Her hair was a mess, her lips parted slightly, her face slack in a way that made something deep in my chest clamp down, hard. My heart dropped. She looked dead. I was losing her, I couldn't allow it. No, not like this.

“She’s not waking up,” Chloe said, voice cracking at the edges. “We tried everything.” That pulled me back. I wasn't letting go. I couldn't.

I was already moving toward the floor.

“Her pulse?” I demanded, reaching for her wrist before either of them could answer. “Still there,” Alex said, voice tight. “But slow. Too slow.”
Fuck.

I pressed my fingers against the inside of Caroline’s wrist. Her skin was too warm, like she’d been burning through whatever the hell she took. The pulse was faint, sluggish.

My jaw locked. She was slipping. Fuck.

I glanced at her desk—half a bottle of wine left open, her laptop still on, highlighters scattered like she’d lost track of what she was doing.

This wasn’t just Adderall and alcohol. I scanned the room fast, eyes landing on Chloe’s purse. Unzipped.

“What do you keep in your purse,” I tilted my head towards the bag. Chloe whitened, “Klonopin”

I muttered. “That’s what she took.” “Oh my God,” she whispered. “She—”
“How many pills were in that bottle earlier?” I cut in.

She blinked at me, slow, unfocused. “I—I don’t know. I don’t count them.”

My jaw ached with the effort of keeping it together. That meant we had no clue how much she took. Which meant I had to act fast.

I pulled out my phone and hit one number.

My brother picked up immediately. Thank fuck for his boring ass MD. “Liam?”
“I need your help,” I said, already pressing a hand against Caroline’s cheek. She didn’t react. Her skin was hot, damp, too still. “19 female, 5’1 or 5’2, maybe 115 pounds, no, wait, she’s been using weight, um 100-105 pounds — fuck, I don’t know. She’s Unresponsive, took Adderall, alcohol, and Klonopin. I don’t know how much. ”

A pause, “How long has she been out?”

“Fifteen, maybe twenty minutes.” Alex and Chloe nodded to confirm the estimated timeline. “Pulse?”
“Slow.” “Respiration?”
I glanced at her chest, counting. Too shallow. Too fucking slow. “Eight, maybe nine per minute.”
A sharp exhale. “You need to keep her awake, Liam. She’s close to respiratory depression.” No shit.
“How do I wake her up?” “Pain response. Sternum rub.”
I was already pushing Chloe aside, rolling Caroline slightly toward me. I pressed my knuckles hard against the center of her chest and—

Nothing.

I ground down harder.

Caroline’s face twitched, her lips parting slightly, a soft whimper slipping out. But her eyes didn’t open.
“Again,” the voice on the phone ordered.

I rubbed harder, my knuckles digging in. Caroline flinched, a small sound escaping, but still
—nothing. A sick feeling bloomed low in my stomach. “She’s not waking up,” I muttered.
“She will. Keep going.”

I pressed down again, harder than before. This time, she whimpered louder, head rolling to the side.

I barely noticed Chloe’s hand covering her mouth, Alex’s grip on the chair tightening. I only saw Caroline.

“Caroline,” I said, voice low, sharp. “Wake up.”

Nothing. Fucking nothing. "Please" I begged silently to any deity that would still listen.

I grit my teeth as something mean and sour knotted in my stomach. I pressed down again, this time hard enough to bruise. Her body jerked slightly, a small, strangled sound escaping her throat. Her eyelids fluttered.

“Come on, sweetheart,” I rasped, voice dangerously soft. “Come back to me.” Another whimper—this time, her brow creased slightly.
Then—a slow inhale.

Her lips parted. Her eyelashes twitched. Finally—finally—her eyes opened.
For a second, she just stared. Unfocused. Heavy.

Then—panic. She tried to lift her arms, but her movements were slow, sluggish, uncoordinated.

“Liam?” Her voice was thin, weak. Confused.

I let out a breath I hadn’t realized I was holding.

I touched her cheek, forcing her eyes back on me. “Yeah, sweetheart,” I murmured. “I’m here.”
She blinked, her pupils still too big, her breathing still too shallow.

I turned back to the phone. “Now what?”

“She’s not out of the woods yet,” Michael responded. “She needs to stay awake for the next few hours. If she stops breathing—”

“She won’t.”

Silence. Followed by, “Keep her conscious. Keep her hydrated. And Liam?” My jaw tightened, sharp and silent, “What?”
“Don’t let her do this again.”

“Don’t mention this to Mom and Dad.” “Wouldn’t dream of it.” the call ended.
I looked back down at Caroline. Her eyes kept trying to close. I tapped her cheek lightly, forcing her to look at me.
“You’re staying awake, baby,” I murmured, my thumb brushing against her skin. She whimpered, trying to move, but her body was too sluggish, too uncoordinated. Chloe and Alex still stood frozen, silent.
I exhaled slowly, shifting my grip, pulling Caroline against my chest. She melted into me too easily.
I pressed my lips to her forehead, voice low, deadly soft. “You scared the shit out of me, sweetheart.”
She didn’t respond, didn’t even flinch.

I glanced at Chloe,“Get me water. Now.”

She nodded quickly, moving. Alex swallowed hard, finally speaking, “Is she going to be okay?”

I looked down at Caroline. Her breathing was still too shallow. Her eyes weren’t fully focused.

I ran a hand down my face, swallowing the rage, the fear, the fucking helplessness. “I’ll make sure of it.”
Chloe returned with some water.

I leaned Caroline against me, steadying her as best as I could. She was dead weight, her body too loose, too unresponsive, but I had no choice—I had to get some fucking fluid in her.

“Caroline,” I murmured, tipping the glass to her lips. “Drink, sweetheart.”

She didn’t respond. Her eyes fluttered half-shut, her body leaning heavily against mine.

“Come on, baby,” I coaxed, my fingers gently stroking her jaw, forcing her focus on me. “Just a little. That’s it.”

She parted her lips slightly, barely reacting when I tilted the glass, letting a few drops slip onto her tongue.

I had to stop myself from cursing. Too slow. Too out of it. I tried again, more insistent. A small sip. Then another.
That was enough, at least for now.

I exhaled through my nose, glancing at Chloe.

“Can you pack a bag for her?” I asked, voice even but firm. “I’m taking her back with me— no use all of us staying up all night.”

Chloe hesitated.

“You can stay here,” she offered. “We can take shifts—” She looked so fucking scared.
I bit down, hard, just to feel something I could control, “I’ll text you updates.” I resolved.

Chloe didn’t look convinced. Alex spoke up instead, quiet but firm. “Are you sure that’s a good idea?”

I didn’t look at her, I couldn’t. If I did, I’d see the doubt—the unspoken I don’t trust you with her right now.

And I wasn’t in the mood to fucking explain myself.

“Yeah,” I said instead, already shifting Caroline’s weight in my arms. “I’m sure.” Chloe swallowed. Then, without another word, she moved to Caroline’s closet.
I turned back to the girl in my arms. She was barely awake. Her breathing was a little steadier, but her limbs were still heavy, useless.

I pressed my lips to her forehead, my grip tightening. “You’re okay,” I muttered, even though I wasn’t sure if that was true yet.

Within minutes, Chloe handed me a small overnight bag.

“I packed a hoodie, some leggings, a toothbrush, whatever else I could grab fast,” she said, voice still too tight.

I nodded, shouldering the bag.

Alex crossed her arms, “Make sure she actually drinks water when she wakes up,” she said. I met her eyes,“I will.”
A beat of silence, Chloe shifted on her feet. “If she gets worse—”
“She won’t.” Chloe exhaled, like she wanted to argue but knew it wouldn’t get her anywhere. Instead, she just nodded.

I adjusted my grip on Caroline, shifting her weight against my chest before standing, lifting her effortlessly.

She barely reacted.

A soft sound escaped her lips, but her body remained slack, her head falling against my shoulder.

I glanced at Chloe and Alex. “Lock the door behind me.”
Then, I turned but before I walked out, almost too casually, “Not a word to anyone. This could easily get back to the Dean and then her life here, her future – it’s all over. None of us want that.” A bleak nod from Alex, Chloe teary eyed next to her meeting my eyes in agreement.

I didn’t slow down, Caroline was mine to handle now, and I’d be damned if I let her spiral out of control again.

By the time I reached my place, Caroline was still barely conscious.

Her body was too limp, her head lolling against my shoulder, breath warm against my collarbone but too slow, too weak.

I didn’t like it, didn’t like how small she felt in my arms, didn’t like how her body barely responded when I shifted her, how she let me carry her like she weighed nothing, didn’t like the fact that she’d let herself get this bad.

I kicked the door open with my foot, stepping inside and immediately locking it behind me. The room was quiet, dim, the only light spilling in from the street below.
I didn’t turn on a lamp.

I just walked straight to the bed, lowering Caroline down slowly, carefully, making sure her head rested against the pillows.

She let out a soft, barely-there whimper, her fingers twitching slightly against the sheets. “Shh,” I murmured, brushing my knuckles over her cheek. “I got you, sweetheart.”
She didn’t react. Her eyes were still half-lidded, pupils blown out, unfocused.

Her body sank into the mattress, her breathing still shallow, her lips parted slightly like she was trying to say something but couldn’t. I sat on the edge of the bed, watching her.

Waiting.

Her breath hitched, barely noticeable, her brows furrowing for just a second before smoothing out again.

I let out a slow breath, running a hand through my hair. She was stable. But she wasn’t okay.
I should have fucking seen this coming. The stress. The schedule. The way she kept pushing herself further and further, like she could force herself to hold everything together through sheer will alone. I should have stopped her before it got this bad. I knew she was losing control. I knew she was using too much Adderall. I knew she wasn’t eating enough, wasn’t sleeping enough. I just didn’t think she’d push herself to the point of fucking this.

Benzos and wine.

That was a line she hadn’t crossed before. And she crossed it alone.

I exhaled sharply through my nose, shaking my head. Not anymore. I wasn’t letting her do this to herself again.

I reached for the water bottle I’d grabbed before leaving the dorm, unscrewing the cap and tilting it to her lips.

“Caroline,” I murmured. “Come on, sweetheart. You need to drink.” She let out a soft, disoriented sound, her head turning slightly away.
I clicked my tongue, slipping a hand under her jaw, guiding her back, “Don’t fight me on this, baby,” I murmured, voice low, coaxing. “Just a sip.”

Her lips parted slightly, breath shallow. I tilted the bottle carefully, letting the water touch her tongue. She swallowed, just barely, but it was something.

“Good girl,” I murmured, my thumb stroking along the curve of her jaw. “Just like that.”

Another tiny sip. Her brows pulled together like she was fighting to focus, fighting to come back to me. I let her swallow again before pulling the bottle back, watching her.

Her eyelashes fluttered slightly.

Her lips moved, barely a whisper, “…Liam?”

A sharp pull in my chest. “Yeah, sweetheart.” My voice was low, steady. “I’m here.”

Her eyes didn’t fully open, but her fingers twitched slightly against the sheets. I caught her hand before she could pull away, my fingers curling around hers.

She was so warm, her pulse still too slow beneath my thumb. I squeezed gently. She exhaled, something barely audible, almost like she was trying to say something else—but then her body slackened again, breath slowing.

No.

No.

She wasn’t drifting off again. I tapped her cheek, firmer this time. “Caroline,” I said, voice sharp, cutting. “Look at me.”
Her eyelids fluttered. I tapped her again, harder, my patience wearing thin. “Open your eyes, baby.”
She made a small, irritated sound, her head shifting.

“Mm,” she mumbled, brows furrowing like she was annoyed. Good. I smirked, despite the tightness in my chest.
“That’s more like it,” I murmured.

Her eyes opened slightly, hazy, unfocused. She tried to turn away, but I didn’t let her. I slid a hand under her jaw, forcing her to keep looking at me.

“You’re staying awake,” I murmured, voice dangerously soft.

She whimpered, trying to move, but her body was too sluggish, too uncoordinated. “Too tired,” she slurred.
My jaw moved on instinct—tight, locked, braced. “I’m not above fucking you to keep you awake,” I said flatly. “You’re not sleeping yet.”

Her brow furrowed again, another soft noise slipping out—something between a protest and a plea. Even so, I didn’t budge. Instead, I tilted her face toward mine, brushing my thumb over her lips, voice low and sharp.

“You scared the shit out of me, sweetheart.”

She blinked, her pupils still too large, her breathing still too shallow.

I didn’t let go of her hand. Didn’t stop watching her. Didn’t let myself relax. Not yet. Not until I was sure she was going to be okay.
I exhaled, slow and controlled, forcing down the anger, the fear, the fucking helplessness. “Talk to me, Caroline,” I murmured. “Keep your eyes on me.”
Her lips parted slightly. She didn’t answer, but she didn’t look away either. For now, that was enough.
The clock on my nightstand blinked back at me, unforgivingly bright. 4:17 AM. Caroline was still awake, even if it were barely.
She was curled against my side, head resting on my chest, her body too warm, too heavy, her limbs still sluggish as she blinked slowly at nothing in particular.

She hadn’t spoken in the last ten minutes. Which meant I was losing her again.
I shifted slightly, adjusting my grip on her waist, and tapped her cheek lightly. “Hey,” I murmured. “Eyes open, sweetheart.”
She made a soft noise, low in her throat, half-protest, half-exhaustion. Her eyelids fluttered, but she didn’t fully wake.

I sighed through my nose, my fingers trailing absentmindedly over her back.

“You don’t get to quit on me yet, baby,” I muttered, my lips brushing against her hair. A slow, slurred mumble from her. “M’not quitting.”
I smirked, even as something tight coiled in my chest.

“Yeah?” I kept my voice low, teasing. “Could’ve fooled me. You look about two seconds from passing out.”

She shifted slightly, curling deeper against me. “Not my fault,” she mumbled. “S’late.”
My grip tightened. “Yeah, well,” I muttered, tapping her cheek again, forcing her lazy gaze back to me. “You should’ve thought about that before you took enough pills to drop a

linebacker.”

She made another soft noise, almost like a pout. I swallowed down the sharp, ugly feeling crawling up my throat. She didn’t even know how fucking close she got. Didn’t even realize how bad it could’ve been. She’d be dead fucking asleep right now if I wasn’t here.

If Chloe hadn’t called me. If I’d been too late.
My jaw tightened, swallowing everything I wanted to keep inside.

I focused on making slow, absentminded circles against her back, keeping my voice light. “You wanna hear a story?”
Her lashes flickered, barely keeping up,“Mm?”

“I figure we gotta keep you awake somehow,” I spoke softly against her. “And you’re not exactly carrying your end of this conversation, sweetheart.”

A slow, lazy blink. “’S’not fair,” she mumbled. “You’re too loud.”

I huffed out a quiet laugh, shaking my head. “Yeah, yeah, I know,” I said, still stroking my fingers down her spine. “Tough luck, baby. You don’t get to sleep yet.”

She made another small, tired noise.

“Fine,” she slurred, her voice weak, distant. “Tell a story.” I exhaled, tilting my head back against the pillow, thinking.
“Alright,” I mused, shifting slightly to hold her closer, to keep her here. “Let’s see… Did I ever tell you about how my brother and I guilted my mom into letting us keep a goldfish?”

A barely-there hum. Her lashes fluttered. “A goldfish?”
I smirked. “A very important goldfish.”

She let out a soft, sleepy breath, her body heavy against mine. My fingers traced slow circles against her ribs, grounding her, grounding me.

“We were little. I was five, Michael was ten, maybe? We got dragged to some shitty local fundraiser—one of those carnivals with barely-functioning rides and rigged games. Mostly boring, but then Michael spotted the fishbowl game. You know, the one where you have to land a ping-pong ball in a tiny glass bowl to win a fish?”

Caroline made a faint sound, barely acknowledging me, but I pressed on, my voice low, steady.

“We were determined,” I murmured. “Must’ve spent twenty dollars each trying to win. And obviously, we did—because we were little shits who refused to lose—but our parents had a strict no-pet policy.”

A small shift against me. A lazy, unfocused tilt of her head. “Why?”
I let out a slow, amused breath, fingers still moving against her skin, coaxing her back every time she drifted too far.

“Who the fuck knows?” I muttered. “But Michael and I weren’t about to give up Fred.” A sluggish blink, “Fred?”
I smirked. “Named after our mom’s dad. There was no way she could get rid of a fish named after her own father.”

A slow, sleepy exhale.

“My parents probably thought Fred would last a few months, tops. But that motherfucker lived for years. Grew massive—practically a koi by the end of it. But when his time finally came, there was a problem.”

Caroline made a soft, questioning sound.

I pulled her just a little closer, keeping my voice gentle, coaxing. “It was winter. The ground was frozen solid. We couldn’t bury him—so we wrapped him in a paper towel and put him in the freezer to wait until spring.”

Another slow blink,“And?”

I huffed out a quiet laugh. “And we forgot about him. For years.” A flicker of something behind her heavy-lidded eyes.
“It wasn’t until a couple of years ago that my mom found him, shoved behind a bag of frozen peas.”

A pause. Then, barely above a whisper—“Why didn’t you just flush him?”

I sucked in a breath, feigning offense,“You don’t flush a fish named after your grandfather down the toilet.”

And then—a tiny, breathy sound. A laugh. Faint, barely there. But real.
Something eased inside me.

She was still with me. Still here. Still mine.

“Atta girl,” I whispered. “Stay with me.”

I pressed a kiss against her temple, my hold tightening around her. “You have a nice laugh,” I said, brushing my thumb along her hip. She huffed out a small breath. “Liar.”
I smirked. “Alright, maybe you sound a little drunk,” I teased. “But I’ll take what I can get.”

She hummed softly, her body settling heavier against me, her fingers barely twitching against my chest.

Silence stretched between us for a second, warm and heavy. I should’ve stopped talking.
I should’ve just let the moment be.

But my jaw tightened, and before I could think better of it— “I was scared, you know.”
Caroline stilled, but didn’t react at first, didn’t answer. Yet, I felt the slightest shift in her breathing. A soft, slow inhale. Then, quietly, she breathed, “Why?”

I swallowed. My grip tightened slightly around her.

“You scared the shit out of me tonight, sweetheart,” I admitted, voice low, raw.

I felt her lazily press her face further against my shirt. Like she was listening -- Not just hearing me. Listening.

I ran a hand through my hair, exhaling slowly. “You could’ve died,” I breathed
The words came out too casual, too soft, like saying them out loud was supposed to make them less real.

But I felt it.

That dark, cold something gnawing at my ribs, curling in my gut, sinking into my bones. Her breathing was soft, steady.
She wouldn’t remember this. That’s why I was saying it.
“I thought about that, you know,” I said softly. “When Chloe called me.”

I swallowed hard.

“I thought—” I let out a slow breath. “I thought maybe I was too late.”

A pause. A long, heavy pause. Caroline’s breath hitched slightly, barely noticeable. I felt her fingers twitch weakly against my chest.

Then—soft, slow, slurred, “You weren’t.”

Something in my throat tightened. I let out a slow breath, my fingers tracing lazy patterns against her back.

“Yeah,” I rasped, “Guess not.” Silence stretched again.
Her breathing was getting lighter, more even: Still awake. Still here.

I pressed my lips against her hair, my voice barely above a whisper, “You’re gonna be fine, sweetheart.”

Caroline breathed out slowly, shifting against me. “Mm,” she hummed. I tightened my grip, more to reassure myself than her.
She wouldn’t remember this in the morning, but I would.


Wednesday Morning

The sun was barely up when Caroline finally fell asleep for real.

I’d kept her awake for hours, kept her drinking water, talking, breathing, but at some point— somewhere between forcing another sip of water past her lips and murmuring quiet reassurances into her hair—she finally gave in. Not to exhaustion, Not to the pills. Just to me.

Her body had relaxed against mine, her breath evening out, her pulse finally normal. She was okay.

I didn’t let go of her right away. I sat there, watching her sleep, my hand still curled around her wrist, my thumb still feeling the faint, steady beat beneath her skin.

I let out a slow breath, rolling my shoulders. Then—carefully, deliberately—I moved.

I slid out from under her, ignoring the pull in my chest when she made a soft, unconscious sound of protest. I tugged the blankets over her, letting my fingers graze her cheek for half a second too long. Then I stood up and got to work.

6:30 AM

By the time Caroline started stirring, I had already decided how the next few days were going to go.

Because last night? That wasn’t happening again. Not on my fucking watch.

I sat at my desk, my laptop open, pulling up her class schedule, her midterm deadlines, her workload. I had been half-listening when she’d stressed over her assignments the past few weeks, filing away details in the back of my mind.

Now, I was putting it all together: When she needed to study; When she needed to eat; When she needed to take a fucking break before she crashed again. I had her entire week mapped out by the time she finally let out a quiet, groggy breath.

I turned in my chair, watching as she slowly shifted under the blankets, her brows pulling together like her body was already protesting being awake.

Then—a sharp inhale. A sudden stiffening. She was remembering. I waited.

She blinked, her eyes still slightly puffy, unfocused, her lips parting. Then her gaze landed on me.

She didn’t say anything at first, didn’t move. She just stared, like she wasn’t sure if she was still dreaming, or maybe having a nightmare.

I leaned forward, elbows resting on my knees, tilting my head slightly. “Morning, sweetheart.”
Her breath caught.

She swallowed. “What…?” Her voice scratched, her throat dry, her words coming out slow.

I was already on my feet, grabbing the water bottle from the nightstand, uncapping it before she could say anything else, “Drink,” I said, voice low but firm.

She blinked at me again. Then—hesitant, confused, still groggy—she sat up slowly, accepting the bottle with shaky fingers. I didn’t take my eyes off her as she took a sip, her hands still trembling. When she lowered the bottle, her breath shaky, I finally spoke again.

“You scared the shit out of me last night, Caroline.”

She froze. Her knuckles went white around the bottle. Her throat moved as she swallowed, like she was trying to push something down, trying to ignore the sharp weight of reality slamming into her chest. I didn’t let her.

“You overdosed in your dorm,” I said, voice steady, even. “Chloe and Alex found you. You weren’t waking up. Chloe called me.”

A tremor ran through her body. I saw the exact moment it clicked, the exact moment the shame hit her. Her fingers tightened around the sheets, her jaw clenching. She didn’t look at

me, didn’t say anything, just stared at the floor, silent. I exhaled slowly, “You’re not doing this again.”
Her gaze snapped back to me, a flicker of something sharp, defensive, too raw. She didn’t argue -- didn’t even try --because she knew.

I let the silence stretch for a beat. Then, I grabbed my laptop, turning it toward her.

Her brows furrowed at the sight of the screen—her schedule, her deadlines, everything I had mapped out for the next week.

“You’ve been running yourself into the ground, sweetheart,” I said, voice calm, even. “And you’re done doing that. You’re following this schedule. No exceptions.”

Her lips parted, followed by her gaze hardening. “You can’t just—” “Yes,” I cut her off. “I can.”
Her mouth snapped shut. A sharp inhale, but she didn’t argue. We both knew she had no control anymore. I had it, and I wasn’t giving it back.

She looked down at her hands, like she was trying to process, like she was deciding how much she wanted to fight me on this.

So I made it easier for her. I pulled something out of my back pocket and held it up. Her fake ID.

She went completely still. Her chest stilled, little hands curled into fists in the blankets. “Liam,” she said slowly, voice shaking just enough for me to hear it.
I tucked it into my wallet before she could say another word. “You don’t need this anymore,” I said simply.
She pulled a breath in with too much effort, her pulse jumping at her throat. I could see it— the tiny, fractured rebellion breaking across her face.

I leaned in slightly, my voice dropping lower, “I’m in charge of your recovery now, Caroline,” I stated quietly, but firmly. “And if you want to keep your life from burning down, you’ll listen to me.”

Her fingers tightened in the sheets.

She looked at me—like she wanted to fight, like she wanted to scream, like she wanted to collapse, but she didn’t do any of those things. She just swallowed. Then, slowly, unwillingly, she nodded.

“And another thing” I reached for her Altoid tin, flipping it open, shaking the pills in my palm.

Her body went rigid. I didn’t even have to say anything. She already knew. “I’m dumping these.”
Her reaction was instant. Panic. Her breath fluttered, sharp, her eyes going wide, frantic. “No—Liam—”
I closed my fingers around the pills, standing up, already moving toward the bathroom. Her entire body jolted, like she was about to lunge out of bed, but her limbs were still too weak, too slow.

“Wait! Wait!”

Her voice broke, desperation laced through it. I stilled, but didn’t turn back – not yet, just waited. I knew what was coming next; I always knew.

“I actually need those,” she said quickly, voice shaking, stumbling over itself.

I could hear it in her breath, the way she was scrambling, trying to buy herself time, trying to find the right words to make me stop.

“I messed up,” she continued, rushing, pleading. “I know I really fucked up. But—what if— what if you keep them?”

I tilted my head slightly, finally glancing back at her.

Her hands were shaking, gripping the sheets like she thought I might actually rip the mattress out from under her.

“I call you every time I need one,” she blurted. “You decide if I do or not.” She was desperate, desperate enough to let me own her addiction, too. “Please,” she whispered. “Don’t just throw them out. I’ll go into withdrawal.”
That. There was the real fear. Not losing the pills, but losing herself without them.

I let the silence stretch for a second, watching her. Her chest rose and fell too fast, her pupils were still too large but not as dilated as when we first found her, her eyes were too glassy, her pulse fluttering in her throat. I rolled the pills in my palm, feeling their small weight.

Then I sighed, shaking my head slightly, “You’re lucky I’m feeling generous, sweetheart.”

Relief slammed across her face so fast, so raw, that for a second—just a second—it almost felt cruel.

I sat back down on the bed, flipping the tin shut with a quiet snap. I slipped it into my pocket.

“Fine,” I murmured. “You call me when you need one.” She nodded quickly, frantically, still breathless from panic.
I tilted my head slightly, voice dropping lower, “But you don’t take a single pill without my say-so. You don’t keep any on you. You don’t touch this tin unless I give it to you myself.”

Another tiny nod, like she was too afraid to even breathe wrong. I smirked.
Checkmate.

“Good girl,” I murmured, brushing my fingers along her jaw before standing. She exhaled shakily, not meeting my eyes.
I turned back to my desk, picking up my laptop, motioning toward her newly organized schedule.

“I emailed you a copy,” I added. “No exceptions.”

She swallowed, her fingers still curled into the sheets. I could see it—the exhaustion, the surrender. She knew she had nothing left to fight with.

No pills.

No alcohol.

No choices.

Just me.

She swallowed again, her voice barely above a whisper. “Okay.”
I smiled.

“That’s my girl.”

Chapter 20: Safe is a Four Letter Word.


I watched as Liam stood by his desk, pushing up the sleeves of his sweater, movements slow, precise, controlled. I hadn’t moved since he took my pills. Since he tucked them into a small black safe, locking away the only thing that had ever made me capable. Now, I sat on his bed, knees pulled to my chest, my pulse steady but too loud, my body aching from exhaustion. My chest hurt, battered and bruised from whatever maneuver Liam had to perform to bring me back to consciousness.

Liam glanced at me, voice low, even. “Are you going to be okay while I shower?” I nodded. Too quickly.
His eyes lingered on me, assessing. Satisfied, or at least willing to let me sit with my own thoughts, he grabbed a towel and turned toward the ensuite bathroom (perks of being team captain). “I’ll be back in ten.”

I swallowed. Ten minutes. The lock clicked behind him.

And then—silence. I let out a breath, slow and shaky, pressing my hands against my thighs, staring at the floor, at nothing, at everything.

I should be dead. The thought was too sharp, too clear. I should be dead, and Liam saved me.
I felt sick, hallow, empty. My head ached, a persistent, dull or throbbing from dehydration and neurochemical crash that reminded me over and over that I shouldn’t have made it.

I squeezed my eyes shut. I had terrified him.
He never admitted fear and yet he repeated it. I scared him, I really scared him. I felt it, too. In the way he held me last night. In the way his grip tightened, just slightly, whenever I shifted or stirred. In the way he hadn’t left my side for even a second until now.

He didn’t trust me alone.

I deserved that. I deserved all of it. I owed him now. Not just for saving me, for not abandoning me.

That’s what anyone else would have done. God, what would my parents have thought?! I could hear my mother’s shrill voice now: “I told you not to burn out; I told you it would be too much for you.” I cradled my head in my hands, feeling the warm tears trail down my cheeks.

Chloe called him for help, when Alex would have dragged me to the hospital.

But Liam—Liam protected me. Liam took care of me. And now?
Now I knew what it meant to be saved by him. Not in a way Chloe would romanticize it, not in a way Alex would dissect it—but in the raw, irreversible sense of being claimed. He didn’t call for help. He became it.

What kind of girl needs her boyfriend abuser to play God in the middle of the night just to keep her from drowning in her own mess?

The answer was ugly. It tasted like iron and desperation in the back of my throat.

I couldn’t afford to unravel again. Not when he’d already picked up every broken piece and protected it as if they were his own. I owed him for that—for not letting me die, for not letting them send me home in shame. I owed him for the future he kept stitched together with his hands around my choices.

So I had to be perfect now, not because he asked, because he saved me to be so.

Because if I ever slipped again—if I ever made him regret the trouble I’d caused—he might finally see it.
That I’m too much. Too messy. Too close to slipping back into someone not worth saving. Too insignificant.

I couldn’t let that happen. I couldn’t lose the one person who looked at my wreckage and decided to keep it.

I dug my nails into my scalp, breathing through the panic, but my thoughts kept spiraling, unraveling, looping back to the one thing I couldn’t stop thinking about—

How the hell am I supposed to do this without Adderall? The exams. The deadlines. The expectations.
How the hell was I supposed to function without it? I tried to remember what was due today, what time my exam was this morning. I couldn’t picture my planner or my notes.

I needed it. Not to get high. Not to feel good. Just to be enough. That’s what they didn’t understand. My head was messy, scattered, chaotic and only Adderall could untangle the web that knotted my thoughts together.

I choked on a breath, pressing the heels of my palms against my eyes, swallowing against the sharp, crawling panic. I was going to fail. I was going to let him down.

“Caroline.”

The panic fractured. I snapped my head up, breath still uneven, pulse still pounding. Liam stood in the doorway, hair damp, towel tied around his waist, watching me.

I didn’t know how long he had been standing there.

His gaze dragged over me, assessing everything at once.

The shaking hands and wet cheeks. The way I had curled in on myself. The way my chest rose and fell too fast.

Softer than I expected—“Breathe.” I did. Because he told me to.
He stepped forward, slow and controlled, like I was something fragile, something delicate. Something that needed him. I hated how badly I wanted to reach for him.

He knelt in front of me, resting his hands on my knees, gentle but grounding. “We’re not doing this,” he murmured.
I blinked at him, my breath still too uneven. “Doing what?” He didn’t answer.
Just tilted his head slightly, studying me as if I were a stunned bird that would take flight at any moment.

I looked away.

He sighed, dragging his hands down my legs, slow and steadying.

“You don’t have to figure this out on your own,” he said, voice dangerously soft. I swallowed. Hard.
I didn’t know what to do with this version of him.

The calm, steady voice. The hands that didn’t hurt. The quiet way he held everything together like it hadn’t been his fingers pulling the strings that made me unravel in the first place. He was the one who cornered me—and now he was the one keeping me upright.

It wasn’t fair, how safe it felt.
How easy he made it to forget why I’d fallen apart in the first place.

Because the truth was, it wasn’t easy. Not really. But here, in this quiet space, it started to feel that way.

And that—that was the part I didn’t know how to survive.

“You’re going to be fine,” he said, voice low, sure, unwavering. “You’re going to get through midterms. You’re going to pass every exam. And you’re going to do it with me.
Understand?”

I let out a shaky breath, my fingers now curling into the sheets, trying to stabilize myself. “I don’t think I can.”
Liam lifted a hand, sliding his fingers through my hair, gripping just enough to make me focus.

“Yes you do. You’re going to listen to I tell you what to do,” he insisted. “and You do it. That’s how.”

I felt lightheaded, unsteady, too fragile to fight him. He tipped my chin up, forcing my gaze back to his.
“We’ll start slow,” he said. “Get dress. We’ll grab breakfast on our way to class. You’ll pass your next exam because you. know. the. material” he emphasized those last four words with an infantilized tap on the nose. “Then we study. You’re not going to crash, because I won’t let you. I’m not giving up and neither are you.”

I stared at him, breathing too hard.

“Why are you being so nice to me?” I whispered, my eyes downcast.

Liam didn’t answer at first. His thumb brushed along my jaw, then stilled. When he spoke, his voice had softened, but the edge was still there—tempered steel under velvet.

“Because I almost lost you.” He said it like a confession, not an excuse. His eyes didn’t waver, didn’t blink, didn’t give me space to look away.
“I told myself I could shape you, guide you, pull you into the life you were meant to have. And I was right. But last night…” He shook his head once, softly sighing through his nose. “Last night proved something else.”

The hand in my hair slid down to cradle the back of my neck, possessive and steady. “Walking away was never an option. Not when I’ve seen how fast you unravel without me.”

He leaned in until our foreheads nearly touched, until there was nowhere to breathe but him. “You asked why I’m being nice,” he murmured. “This isn’t me being nice, Caroline. This is me making sure you live long enough to belong to me the way I always planned.”

His grip tightened—not painful, just certain. “You don’t get to give up.” “Okay.” My voice softer than a whisper.
A flicker of satisfaction flashed through his eyes.

Then—softer, but still final: “Good girl.” He kissed my forehead and something in my chest eased.

The panic was still there, but it has lessened to make room for another emotion I couldn’t quite name.

Because Liam had a plan. I didn’t have to do this alone. He was going to take care of everything. For now, that was enough.

Liam didn’t let me sit in bed for long.

The moment my breathing evened out, the moment my panic quieted under his hands, he was pulling away—not far, not completely, but just enough to shift the energy between us.

To remind me what came next.

“Up,” he murmured, standing. “We’re not going to be late.”

Late. My exam. The normalcy of it hit me all at once, like I had been thrown forward in time. Like last night hadn’t happened. Like I hadn’t woken up on the edge of breaking.

I stared at him, blinking too fast, my body still heavy, still slow, struggling to keep up with how easily he had moved on.

Liam sighed through his nose, reaching out, gripping my wrist, pulling me up before I could even try to stand on my own. I let him. My body folded into him too easily, and his arm was already there, steadying me, supporting me. He didn’t say anything, didn’t acknowledge the way I leaned on him. He just held me up, made sure I was steady, then released me.

Calm, certain, no room for argument: “Clothes are in the bathroom.” I hesitated, just for a second.
“Chloe packed them,” answering the question before I could ask. “I told her what you’d need.”

The words landed strange—not bad, not aggressive, but final. He had handled it. I exhaled, nodded. Stepped past him toward the bathroom.
I told myself I wasn’t following orders just because he instructed me.

I told myself I wasn’t grateful that he had already figured out everything I would need.

The clothes Chloe had packed were familiar, but when I pulled them on, something about them felt wrong.

The leggings were too loose. The sweatshirt was worn in, soft, but it didn’t feel like armor the way it usually did. Maybe it was because my hands were still shaking, or maybe it was because I had almost died last night.

Or maybe it was because I knew Liam had decided what I’d wear today before I even got the chance.

I exhaled, pressing my hands against the cold edge of the sink, staring at my reflection.

My face looked too pale, too hollow, like I hadn’t fully come back into my body yet. My eyes were dry and bloodshot. I didn’t recognize myself. I looked like a junkie.

How could Liam want this? I wasn’t perfect, he had to have seen it by now. I would be free of him by the end of midterms, he just wouldn’t dump me before then. He would be too afraid of another overdose… or worse.

I swallowed.

A dark thought invaded my consciousness: if he let go, could I survive? Would he ever let me go or would he chose to ruin me instead - soil me so I would have no prospects or future here. Would he be that cruel? I could see Ethan being that ruthless but Liam?

Liam hated embarrassment— if I embarrassed him, he would do anything to save face. I knew what I had to do, I had to pull myself together. He deserved it, he saved me.

Without thinking, I reached for my bag: the Altoid tin, my fucking lifeline. I barely got my fingers into the pocket before I remembered. It wasn’t there. It was in his safe.

My breath hitched. Not panic. Not yet. Just…awareness. A sharp, undeniable reminder that I had given him the key to my survival.

I let my hand drop. Took a breath. Stepped out of the bathroom.

Liam was already waiting by the door, scrolling through something on his phone, looking completely at ease. Like this was just another morning. Like he hadn’t just saved and improved my life.

He glanced up when he heard me, and something in his expression shifted. He didn’t say anything.

Didn’t comment on the way I hesitated in the doorway, like I wasn’t sure if I should wait for him to tell me what to do. Instead, he reached toward the desk and picked up a coffee cup.

Held it out to me. “Drink.”
I stepped forward, taking it without thinking. The warmth seeped into my hands, grounding. Liam watched me take the first sip, then—like he had been waiting for that exact moment— he held out his other hand. Something small, something white, familiar. I inhaled sharply.

My pill. I stared at it, then at him. Liam raised a brow, patient, expectant. “Go on.”

The breath I didn’t realize I had been holding rushed out of me. I took it and swallowed it with another sip of coffee.

For the first time since waking up, something inside me settled. The corner of Liam’s mouth curved upward.
“See?” he murmured, reaching out to tuck a strand of hair behind my ear. “Liam” my voice was soft.
“Yeah?” He studied me.

I played with the sleeve of my sweatshirt. “Can I wear one of your hoodies instead?”

His smile grew, “Of course.” He pulled one from his closet, black, nondescript but on me, it was obviously his. I curled my fingers around the collar, breaking in the distinct scent. His: spice, woodsy, the lingering note of bergamot.

“It looks better on you.” He slid his fingers beneath the fabric, gently tugging my hair loose, each movement unhurried, deliberate. I felt the warmth of his hands that lingered just a second too long.

“You’ll be just fine, sweetheart. I’m going to make sure of it.”

And he was right, at least about the first exam of the day. I was tired, but it was easier than I had expected. When I exited the lecture hall, Liam was waiting for me, text book in hand as he studied for his own exam.

He tucked me under his arm, and walked me to the library, keeping me close to him. The walk was short but not without a question of how my exam went or explaining the schedule for the day: how many hours to work on a midterm paper due by midnight, how many hours to study for the exam tomorrow, when we’d be breaking for lunch and to stop back to clothing and the rest of my books to last me the next few days.

The library was still waking up when we arrived, the morning light streaming through the tall arched windows in golden beams. Most of the campus was quiet, students hovering over oak desk, typing frantically away on papers due during midterm week. Liam had insisted we get here early between classes, to get the best seat. My head still ached, but the pain had dull; it was enough to be noticeable but not distracting and for that, I was grateful.

“Finish that essay,” Liam said as we slid into a cozy corner table by the window. He pulled his laptop out of his bag, setting it on the table with practiced ease.

“I’m working on it,” I said defensively, dropping my bag onto the floor and fishing out my notes.

“Yeah and you’ve been saying that for a week,” he said, raising an eyebrow at me. “How many pages do you even have?”

“Three,” I muttered, avoiding his gaze.

“Three,” he repeated, leaning back in his chair with a faint smirk. “Out of ten.” “I’ve been busy,” I shot back.
“You’ve been avoiding it,” he said, his voice calm but pointed. “And now you’re finishing it.”

I sighed, methodically setting up my notebook and laptop. “You’re so bossy,” I muttered under my breath.

“You love it,” he said casually, scrolling through his own screen.

I didn’t respond, but the heat rising to my cheeks probably gave me away. I focused on the blank document in front of me, the cursor blinking like it was mocking me.

“What’s the topic again?” Liam asked after a moment, leaning across the table to glance at my notes.

“It’s for my political science class,” I said, shifting uncomfortably. “Voter behavior in swing states.”

He snorted. “Exciting.”

“It’s harder than it sounds,” I said, glaring at him.

He grinned, unbothered. “Let me see what you’ve got.”

I hesitated, my fingers curling around the edge of my notebook. “Why?”

“Because,” he said, reaching across the table and spinning my laptop around to him. “You clearly need help and I happen to remember the topic from when I took the same class.”

“Liam—”

“Relax,” he said, scrolled through document. “I’m not going to rewrite it for you. But—” He stopped, pointing to one of my bullet points. “This. You’re overcomplicating it.”

“I’m not overcomplicating it,” I said defensively.

“Yes, you are,” he said firmly. “You don’t need to write a whole paragraph about voter psychology here. Just focus on the statistics.”

I stared at him, frowning. “You’re a hockey player. How do you know so much about this?” He smirked, leaning back in his chair. “I pay attention. You should try it sometime.”
I rolled my eyes but couldn’t help the small smile tugging at my lips. “Fine. What else?”

He leaned forward again, his finger tracing down the scren. “Your conclusion. It’s too vague. You need something stronger, something that ties everything together. Like…” He paused, his gaze sharpening. “Here -You’re arguing that voter behavior is influenced by socioeconomic factors, right? So end with that. Make it clear.”

I blinked at him, surprised. “That’s… actually helpful.” “Don’t sound so surprised,” he said, his smirk widening.
I laughed softly, shaking my head. We worked in silence for a while after that, but it wasn’t the tense silence I’d expected. Liam occasionally glanced up from his laptop to check on me, his gaze lingering just long enough to make me self-conscious. But he didn’t push, didn’t demand more than I was willing to give. It was… nice.

As I finished the last paragraph, I sat back in my chair, stretching my arms above my head. “Done,” I said, letting out a long breath.

“See?” Liam said, grinning. “That wasn’t so hard.” “Easy for you to say,” I muttered, closing my laptop.
“Hey, I’m the one who made it easier for you,” he said, his tone teasing. “Fair enough,” I said, rolling my eyes. “Thanks. I guess.”
He laughed, standing and stretching. He glanced at his watch “Come on,” he said, holding out his hand. “Let’s take a break.”

I hesitated, glancing at the schedule on my agenda. “But—” I remembered the last time he demanded a break and what he took from me. I didn’t have it in me for another one of his study breaks, not like that.

“No buts,” he said, grabbing my hand and pulling me to my feet. “You’ve been staring at that screen for hours. You need to move.”

He didn’t wait for me to argue, leading me through the rows of bookshelves, far from the study rooms, until we reached a small alcove tucked away in the corner of the library. It was quiet here, the hum of the main floor fading into the background.

“I’m not fucking you between bookshelves,” I stated with too much confidence.

“Obviously not, sweetheart. It’s too crowded and I don’t want an audience,” he scoffed. From above me, he grabbed a random book from the shelf. “Here you go.”

I glanced at the title and laughed. “It’s a cookbook.”

He flipped through it, grinning. “Exactly. Let’s see if you’re as bad at cooking as you are at essay writing.”

I rolled my eyes, grabbing the book from him and shoving it back onto the shelf. “You want me to cook you dinner?” He pulled it back out, tucking it under his arm.

“Don’t you think you owe me for saving your life?” he teased, stepping closer, his grin softening. I felt a pang of guilt but his eyes were too soft, too playful.

“Glad to know you’re no longer concerned about me poisoning you,” I said, our banter made my chest tighten.

He reached out, brushing a strand of hair behind my ear, his touch lingering for just a moment too long. “You did really well today,” he said softly. “I’m proud of you.”

My breath caught, the words settling over me like a blanket. It wasn’t something I was used to hearing, and the sincerity in his voice made it even harder to process.

“Thanks,” I said quietly, unable to meet his gaze.

“I mean it. Not everyone could perform like…” he said, his tone shifting to something more serious. He cleared his throat “like last night didn’t happen.” I stiffened. I couldn’t keep his gaze as he spoke. The weight of reality crashed over me and I couldn’t look at him when that heaviness pulled me down.

“Hey, don’t get all shy on me now. You’ve got to keep up with me, remember?” he cupped my cheek.

I forced a laugh, shaking my head. “I’ll try.”

“Good,” he said, his grin widening. “Because we’ve got a lot more work to do.”

Liam leaned against the bookshelf, crossing his arms as he watched me. The warm light filtering through the nearby window softened the sharpness in his features, but his eyes still carried that familiar intensity. It was as though he saw more of me than I was comfortable sharing, even in moments like this.

“So,” he said, tilting his head slightly, “now that the essay is out of the way, what’s next on your list of excuses?”

I raised an eyebrow. “Excuses?”

“Yeah,” he said, smirking. “You’re always finding reasons to doubt yourself. What’s the next one going to be? ‘I’m not good enough’? ‘I need more time’? ‘Maybe Liam’s just too smart, and I can’t keep up’?”

I massaged my temples, trying to relieve the pounding in my head, “Don't be a jerk.”

He shrugged, his smirk softening into something more genuine. “Last time I checked, I’m a knight in shining armor. But I’m not wrong.”

I opened my mouth to argue, but his gaze held mine, steady and unyielding, and the words died in my throat. I was suddenly more aware of the bruise blossoming on my chest, no doubt

getting uglier by the hour.

He wasn’t wrong about any of it. I did doubt myself constantly, part of me felt like he enjoyed pointing it out, like it gave him some twisted sense of satisfaction to watch me squirm. Yet then there was the other part—the part that recognized the rare sincerity in his words, the part that wanted to believe him when he said I was capable.

“I’m just saying,” he continued, his voice softer now, “you’ve got this habit of getting in your own way. But I see through it. You’re smarter than you give yourself credit for.”

I crossed my arms, leaning back against the opposite shelf. “You don’t have to keep saying that, you know. I get it.”

“Do you?” he asked, his tone light but probing. “Because I’m not sure you do.”

I frowned, unsure how to respond. The silence stretched between us, broken only by the faint hum of voices and footsteps in the distance. Finally, I sighed, looking down at the floor.

“I don’t know,” I admitted.

“I know,” he said, his voice resembling something kind. “Imposter syndrome, but you worked just as hard to get here as anyone else, harder even. It’s not like they give out a lot of scholarships around here – gotta keep the elites elite, right?”

“If you say so,” I conceded though I wasn’t convinced.

He stepped closer, his presence filling the small space between us. “Caroline,” he said, his tone firmer now. “You’re here because you deserve to be here. Stop thinking otherwise.”

I looked up at him, startled by the conviction in his voice. My eyes locked on his, studying them for deception or malice. I saw none. I remember reading that blue could convey feelings of trust and honesty and as I looked into his blue eyes, I wanted so much to take his words as gospel. So for a moment, I let myself believe him. I let myself imagine that he was right, that I wasn’t some fluke or a fraud. That I could belong here—belong anywhere—without constantly questioning it.

“And for the record,” he added, his lips quirking into a small smile, “I wouldn’t waste my time on someone who wasn’t worth it.”

My chest tightened, the warmth of his words settling over me like a blanket. “You’re really good at this, you know,” I said, trying to keep my voice light.

“At what?”

“At… saying the right thing. Making me believe it.” He grinned, leaning closer. “That’s because it’s true.”
I rolled my eyes, but I couldn’t fight the smile tugging at my lips. “You’re impossible.”

“And you love it,” he said, his voice low and teasing.

I laughed, the sound echoing softly in the quiet alcove. “You keep saying that word but I don’t think you know what it means .”

“Doesn’t matter,” he said, stepping back just enough to give me space. “But you’re stuck with me either way.”

I shook my head, still smiling, and reached for my bag. “Come on,” I said. “Let’s get back to work.”

“Not so fast,” he said, fingers wrapping gently around my wrist. “There’s one more thing.” I blinked at him, wary. “What?”
He reached into his pocket and pulled out a small envelope—ivory, thick, monogrammed in the corner. My name was written across the front in his handwriting.

I raised an eyebrow but took it, “This better not be some weird love letter.” “Open it,” he said, smug now, arms crossed like he already knew how I’d react.
I slid my finger beneath the flap, unfolding the paper inside. At first glance, it looked like a receipt. Then my eyes caught the name of the restaurant—Monarch & Rye—followed by the time and date. Next Friday. 8:30 PM. The same day midterm grades were due to be posted.

Beneath it, one line written in his unmistakable scrawl:
Once you see how well you did on your exams, change into that little black dress I love so much. I’ll handle the rest.

I looked up at him, a small smile tugging at my mouth despite myself. “What’s this?” “Don’t act so surprised,” he said, stepping closer. “I can be thoughtful when I want to be.”
I laughed softly, turning the envelope over between my fingers. “Is this a reward or just another test?”

He dipped his head, voice low near my ear, “It’s a reward, sweetheart. But you don’t get to keep it unless you earn it.”

The heat of his words lingered against my skin longer than they should have. I swallowed, pulse tripping.
“Guess we should get back then,” I whispered. He nodded—sharp, knowing. “Yeah, I guess so.”

Before dinner, we headed back to my dorm to pick up more clothes and any other books I had left behind. Chloe squeezed me too tight. Alex held me too long. Liam shifted uncomfortably.

I had expected pointed words from Alex, panic from Chloe but both supported – no encouraged – me to spend the next few days at the hockey house. They meant under surveillance, but I couldn’t blame them. Going back to the dorm, to the scene of the crime – it made me nauseous. Maybe after midterms, I wouldn’t have such a visceral reaction, but this week, I didn’t need another reason to doubt myself.

Chapter 21: Don't Ruin This.


She impressed me.
Not because she was strong—not in the way the world defines it—but because she bent without breaking, yielded without disappearing. The way she pushed when others would have unraveled. The way she followed when her pride must’ve begged her to resist. That’s what impressed me.

She didn’t fight me. Not anymore. And that’s how I knew she’d make it.

I’d been so focused on her—on managing the pacing, the schedule, her spiraling—I'd barely touched my own work load until the early morning hours of Thursday. My mind was too wired to sleep, though my body relied on Mason’s energy drinks. Admittedly, the papers weren’t due Friday, but I wanted them off my plate so I could focus on what was more important. I hit submit once satisfied that it would be enough to ace the class. Polished.
Perfect. Efficient, as always.

Now, in the quiet just before dawn, I sat at the edge of the bed and watched her sleep. Her face slack in the dim light of my laptop screen, mouth slightly parted, brow smooth for the first time all week. The shadows under her eyes were still dark, her color still off. Her breathing had evened out, though, her pulse steady. I tried to memorize the rhythm.

I leaned forward slightly, elbows on my knees, letting my gaze drag across her features, cataloging every rise and fall of her chest, every twitch of her fingers curled beneath my hoodie sleeves. She wore it to bed without asking. I didn’t correct her. She looked small again. Not delicate—she was never delicate, not really. But her she rested stripped down. Post-collapse. Mid-reconstruction. She had no idea how much better she performed when she surrendered. I set an alarm for later that morning and crawled into bed next to her, pulling her gently to me, anchoring her against my chest like it was the only place she ever stayed whole.

The worst had passed. Two exams left—one today, one tomorrow. She still needed rest, but she needed structure more. I kept her schedule tight: review, clean food, movement, praise. Now she was in the kitchen making dinner—something simple, something she from the cookbook we checked out earlier, though we both knew she was doing it for me. In the next room, I let myself play a round of pool with Mason and Ethan, ears still half-tuned to the sound of her moving behind the stove.

Ethan sank his shot with lazy precision, cue balanced in one hand like it was just another accessory to whatever performance he’d decided on tonight. He didn’t look up when he said it—just loud enough for me to hear:

“She’s cooking again. You really do have her trained. Another meal, and I might be tempted to call it ‘Stockholm’”

I didn’t react.

He glanced toward the kitchen, where the soft clatter of pans blended with the scent of garlic and steam. Caroline had insisted on making something simple from the cookbook we borrowed yesterday—smelled like chicken parm.

“You know what this reminds me of?” Ethan said, moving around the table. “Those girls in boarding school who got real docile after a long weekend. They came back different—tighter smiles, straighter posture, and a whole lot more ‘yes, sir.’ You knew someone finally laid down the law.”

Mason straightened where he leaned against the far wall, expression tightening. “That’s not funny.”

Ethan shrugged, “Wasn’t a joke.”

I chalked my cue in silence, eyes on the table. I knew what Ethan was doing—he was circling. He hadn’t seen the collapse, hadn’t seen her unconscious in my bed, hadn’t had to force water between her lips while her pulse dragged. But he knew something. He always did.

Mason frowned, arms folded. “Has she said anything? I mean, I don’t want to pry, but… she’s just been different lately. Quieter, more distracted. Like something’s off.”

“She’s fine,” I said flatly.

Mason hesitated. “Did something happen?”

“She’s tired,” I replied. “Midterms. Pressure. She’s handling it.”

Ethan hummed, leaning over for his next shot, eyes flicking to me and not the cue ball. “She’s doing more than handling it. She’s, dare I say, pliant? Obedient even. That’s new.”

The ball cracked against the side pocket, “You should be proud,” he added, stepping back. “It takes a certain kind of discipline to turn panic into submission.”

I turned slowly, cue in hand, jaw tight. “Whatever happened earlier this week doesn’t leave this fucking room.”

Ethan’s lips tightened, “Relax. I’m not the one who’s going to talk.”

Mason looked between us now, brows pinched. “Wait. What happened this week?” I shot Ethan a warning glance. He knew better. His eyebrows shot up in surrender.
“She had a moment,” I said, turning my back on them as I lined up my shot. “It’s handled.” Mason gripped his cue tighter, “A moment?”
“She’s fine now.”

Ethan whistled low. “Handled it,” he repeated. “That’s one way to say it.”

I took the shot. Sank it clean. Then stood, “Say whatever the fuck you want, Ethan. Just don’t say it about her.”

