Dozens of tabs still open. News from every direction—left, right, international, obscure, even one from a Russian substack I’d never heard of. Articles about government policy, protest movements, censorship. Headlines about collapsed states, corrupted courts, natural disasters and slow-burning wars. Even some searches about my family: my dad’s senate history, my mom’s foundation work, some old tabloids about Michael that stained his otherwise perfect veneer.

I scrolled through them, tapping one after the next, watching the puzzle pieces form.

She wasn’t just trying to stay informed. She was trying to orient herself—to find something objective in a world that didn’t offer clarity anymore. She wanted context. Truth. A signal that someone, somewhere, was still playing fair.

She hadn’t realized yet: fairness was never the point. The people who win are the ones who write the rules.

I opened her messages last. Chloe first—row after row of sunshine-washed texts, filled with inside jokes and heart emojis, her relentless optimism bleeding through the screen like a perfume she didn’t know how to wash off.

Chloe: You’ve got this Carly!! You’re going to KILL that exam!

Chloe: Midterms are temporary, red wine is forever [wine glass emoji]

Chloe: Liam is a legitimate knight in shining armor! Please marry him for me!

I let those scroll past. Chloe’s fantasy didn’t bother me. If anything, it helped.

Mason was next. Sparse, straightforward. A few reminders about the paper due next week. A strange link to that indie book about a man in heart-printed boxers fighting space lizards with his talking cat that he won’t shut up about.

Mason: It’s actually about trauma, believe it or not. Worth reading if you can get past the weird cover.

I raised an eyebrow, reread the message, then tucked that away. I didn’t bother replying. She never talked about Mason much, but that still didn’t mean I wanted them bonding over anything.

Her mother’s name was just “Mom,” but the tone was unmistakable. Seemingly normal conversations with a a sprinkling of passive-aggressive jabs wrapped in moral superiority every few lines.

Mom: Saw the Dean’s List announcement. Finally putting that scholarship to use?

Mom: Have you gained weight? You look puffy in the photo you posted with your roommate.

Mom: I don’t like how Liam is front and center in your life these days. It seems like he’s always around – are you sure he doesn’t see you as a charity case?

The kind of slow-burn cruelty that made itself known in pinpricks, not slaps. The kind that left no visible bruises. I read every word. Not because I needed to, but because I wanted to see what had shaped her before I took over.

Then Ethan. His name in all caps, no emoji, no heart, just ETHAN—as if the phone itself wanted to warn her.

Ethan: Still alive, princess? Tell your boyfriend I found your planner.

Ethan: Cute goals. Do you get a gold star when you reach orgasm, or just extra flashcards?

Ethan: Don’t worry, I won’t tell him you stared at me longer than five seconds today. Probably means nothing. Right?

I didn’t react. Ethan always pushed too far. That was his game. But even he knew where the line was—because if he didn’t, I’d show him.

Next was the text chain from that guy in her class that had asked her to be in his group. He had texted her twice since she gave him her number.

Jon: We'd like to meet the week after midterms and then once after thanksgiving. All of us are free right after class on Tuesdays. Would that work for you?

Jon: I know you're probably busy studying for midterms, but wanted to see if those two Tuesdays would work?

She hadn't responded. I decided to help.

Caroline: Hey, sorry. Midterm studying took priority. I took a look at my other courses' syllabi and I won't have the bandwidth to join. I'm sorry I didn't respond sooner.

Jon wrote back almost immediately.

Jon: Damn, I could have really used your help. Oh I'm sure you could have.
Jon: And this doesn't have to do with some over protective boyfriend?

I deleted that message and continued my research.

Finally, I opened our thread. My messages. Our archive. The record of her unraveling.

Eat something before your 10AM. Don’t make me text Chloe again. You looked stunning. Try not to make the TA flinch when you speak.
No, you didn’t miss the reading—you absorbed it better than half the idiots in that room. That eye roll was unnecessary. You know I’m right.
I know you think you’re subtle, but I saw the way you looked at me during lunch. Next time, don’t pretend.
Keep going. You’re almost through. You’ll thank me later. You were made for this.
Good girl.
I’m proud of you

I flipped through the photos we had sent back and forth to one another. The one I had changed to my profile picture, the first one she posted of us on Instagram. The photo I took of her studying, the one I caught of her laughing. I still have saved the picture of all the hoodies she had acquired from me within our first six weeks together (so much so that we had to create a rotation rule of how many she could keep in her dorm at a time).

I closed her phone and set it gently back beside her notes, just as her brow furrowed again in sleep, her fingers twitching toward something invisible.

I watched her a while longer—this girl who devoured information like it could save her, who smiled at Chloe’s delusions, tolerated Mason’s chaos, flinched from her mother’s poison, and pretended not to understand Ethan’s threats. She curled into herself like a secret, and somehow, still believed she had parts I hadn’t seen.
But I had. I had seen all of her. And soon, she’d see all of me.
I realized then, we’d be ok. More than ok, this had been a hard week and I could already see
it behind us. I stroked her cheek softly with the back of my knuckles, careful not to wake her and let myself focus again on what was important: Us.




Chapter 23: Signed, Sealed, Still Dangerous.





I refreshed the page again, my stomach twisting as the screen flickered. The numbers sat there, unmoving, bold against the white background. All As. All damn near perfect.

I stared at it, my breath shallow. I’d known it would be high. I hadn’t doubted for a second— not really. Liam wouldn’t have let me fail. But seeing it confirmed, right there in black and white, sent a shiver through me.

Chloe made a noise next to me, half a groan, half a sigh. “Well, at least I didn’t bomb it.” I glanced over as she scrolled down her screen.
“B-minus average,” she muttered. “Not great, but it could be worse.”

“That’s solid,” I said, my voice automatic. My fingers were still curled over the edge of my laptop, pressing into the metal like I needed to ground myself.

Chloe scoffed. “Not compared to you.” She leaned over, peering at my screen, and her eyes widened. “Holy shit, Caroline. I’ve never so many As!”

I swallowed. Nodded.

Chloe let out a low whistle. “Damn. Top grade?” I hesitated. Nodded again.
For a second, she just stared at me, then broke into a grin. “I knew all that studying would pay off.” She nudged me with her elbow, playful, oblivious to the way my pulse spiked at the word. “And I knew Liam helping you was a good thing.”

My throat felt tight. “He didn’t—” I started, but she cut me off with a smirk.

“Don’t even try it. I’ve seen you two. I mean, yeah, he’s intense, but he clearly wants you to succeed.”

I didn’t answer.

Because how could I explain that it wasn’t just studying? That it wasn’t about me succeeding, not really. It was about proving myself to him. It was about making sure I didn’t disappoint him. About the unspoken consequences that lurked beneath his words.

Chloe sighed, stretching her arms over her head. “Honestly, it’s kind of sweet. He’s really invested in you, you know? Like, not just as you but in you. It’s refreshing.”

A small, bitter laugh built in my chest, but I swallowed it down. She had no idea.
She had no idea that he never gave me a choice. That if I hadn’t gotten this grade, I wouldn’t be sitting here right now, scrolling through scores like any normal student. I’d be somewhere else, somewhere alone with Liam, listening to his disappointment, feeling it settle in my bones.

And yet…

These grades were mine. The knowledge was mine.

Liam had put it there, forced it into me, but it was still mine. Chloe tilted her head, studying me. “You’re weirdly quiet.” “I’m just… processing,” I said finally.
She grinned. “Processing how ridiculously smart you are?” I huffed out a breath, shaking my head.
“No, but seriously,” she continued. “You and Liam together? Power couple material.” My heart clenched.
Power couple.

The words should have felt good. Maybe, in another life, they would have. But Liam wasn’t my partner. He was my standard. My judge. My consequence.

Still, I forced a smile. “Yeah,” I murmured. “Something like that.” Chloe beamed, satisfied, and turned back to her laptop.
I let out a slow breath and finally closed my screen.

Power couple. More like power imbalance.

And yet, I couldn’t ignore the truth clawing at the edges of my mind. I wouldn’t have gotten a 4.0 without him.
And that scared me more than anything. Liam texted me.
Of course he did; grades had been released campus-wide. He expected to know. He deserved to know—at least, in his mind.

Liam : so what’s the verdict?

I hesitated, my fingers hovering over the screen. The AS still burned in my mind, both an achievement and a weight pressing against my ribs. I could already hear his voice, the cool expectation, the way he’d already assumed I hadn’t failed.

Caroline : passed

A few seconds passed. Then another text.

Liam : yeah I know that. What grades?

My throat tightened. Just tell him. Get it over with.

Caroline : All As

It was true. Across the board, all A’s. A couple low ones, but still safe. Still acceptable. A beat. Then—
Liam : what about that poli sci paper ?

I exhaled slowly, my grip tightening on my phone.

He didn’t care about the other grades. Of course, he didn’t. This was the one that mattered. The one he had made sure I wouldn’t fail.

The one I had no choice but to get right.

Caroline : 20/20

I watched the screen, the small typing bubble appearing almost immediately. Then disappearing. Then appearing again.

My chest tightened.

Finally—

Liam : that’s my girl.

The words sent a shiver through me. A strange mix of pride and relief and something I didn’t want to name.

Then—

Liam : knew you wouldn’t disappoint me. My stomach twisted. There it is.
I should have been happy. I was happy. But also… there was that familiar, suffocating sensation curling at the edges of my mind.

I texted back, fingers moving before I could overthink it.

Caroline : You doubted me?

The typing bubble appeared again. This time, he took his time.

Liam : No. But you did. My breath caught.
Liam : You worry too much, baby. I told you—when you listen to me, you win. My fingers went slack against my phone.
I should have been angry. Should have resented the way he spoke, the way he took ownership of my success like it belonged to him. But the worst part was…

He was right.

Without him, I wouldn’t have gotten the 98. I hated that truth.
But I couldn’t deny it.

Liam : I’m excited for Monarch and Rye I didn’t respond.
I stared at the screen, willing myself to type something—anything. But my fingers stayed still, my breath shallow.

I should feel good. This was a good thing. I had worked hard—he had made sure of that— and I had done exactly what he expected of me. So why did I feel like I had just stepped onto unsteady ground?

The phone buzzed again in my hand.

Liam : wear your hair down. I’ll pick you up at 8. No questions. Just expectation.
That was Liam.

