It didn’t matter that I hadn’t answered.

Liam would take my silence as compliance. He always did.

Chloe cocked her head, studying me. “Please tell me you’re not upset that you didn’t get a perfect score in every single class.”

I relaxed my jaw, realizing I had been clenching it. My muscles ached from the tension I hadn’t noticed creeping in.

“Seriously, ,” she continued, throwing her hands up in exasperation. “I would sell my soul for just half of your score card.”

I forced a small laugh, shaking my head as if to brush it off. “I know. I’m not upset.” But the words felt like a lie even as I said them.
Chloe narrowed her eyes. “Then why do you look like someone just kicked your puppy?” I exhaled, forcing my shoulders to loosen. “I don’t. I’m just tired.”
Chloe didn’t look convinced, but she let it go, leaning back against the couch with a dramatic sigh. “If I had your grades, I’d be dancing on this coffee table right now.” She glanced at me. “You should be celebrating, you know. And not just because Liam thinks so.”

I felt the twist of a knife at the mention of his name. She didn’t know.
She didn’t know that celebrating wasn’t really an option—not when it was an expectation, not when Liam had already decided what the night would look like.

“Speaking of,” she went on, nudging me with her foot. “He must be over the moon, right?” I hesitated for a fraction of a second too long.
Chloe’s eyes sparkled with something teasing. “Don’t even try to act like he didn’t text you the second grades dropped.”

I rolled my eyes, reaching for my phone as if I wasn’t already acutely aware of the messages sitting there. “Of course he did.”

She smirked. “And? Was he proud of you?”

Proud . The word stuck in my throat. He’d said That’s my girl. He’d told me he knew I wouldn’t disappoint him. But had he actually said he was proud of me?

I swallowed, forcing a nod. “Yeah. He was happy.”

Chloe grinned. “Duh. I bet he’s already planning some big celebration.” I didn’t answer, because yes, he was - and he’d already picked my dress.
Chloe’s smile softened, her voice turning warm. “See? He really cares about you.”

I swallowed, the words landing oddly in my chest. Cares. That wasn’t the word I would’ve chosen.

Liam expected this. He had trained me for this. Was that the same as caring? I forced a smile, nodding as if I agreed.
Chloe didn’t seem to notice my hesitation. “You’re lucky, you know. Some guys wouldn’t bother. But Liam? He is actively invested in your success.”

I felt a knot in my middle tighten.

Push. He pushed. But what Chloe didn’t see—what no one saw—was what happened when I didn’t move fast enough.

I dropped my gaze to my hands, pressing my nails into my palms to keep them steady. Chloe sighed happily, stretching her arms over her head. “Well, have fun. You deserve it.” I bit the inside of my cheek, nodding again.
Deserve .

Another word that didn’t feel quite right.



I dragged the curling iron through the last section of my hair, watching as it settled into soft waves. I told myself it was just part of my routine, that I wanted to look nice for me, that it had nothing to do with him.

But then why did I keep checking to make sure the waves looked exactly how he liked them?

I reached for my brush, smoothing out any imperfections, my hands steady yet my thoughts anything where anything but. The black dress fit well enough. It fit me better a month or two ago. The fabric clung in places I hadn’t expected, and I found myself tugging at the hem,

fussing with the neckline. The necklace he had bought me was secured around my neck, completing the look perfectly.

But I had lost weight. I knew it. Chloe knew it. She had stopped making comments about my appetite, but I wasn’t stupid. I saw the way her gaze lingered when I skipped meals or when my jeans sat looser on my hips.

Behind me, she sat cross-legged on my bed, scrolling through her phone. But when I reached for my lipstick, she spoke.

“You know you still look beautiful, right?” I stilled. “What?”
Chloe tilted her head, offering me a small smile. “I know you’ve been feeling… different lately. But you’re still you, Carly. You’re still beautiful.”

I turned back to the mirror, smoothing my hand down my waist. Is that what I was feeling? Different? Or was it something else entirely?

I had been avoiding my own reflection lately, but tonight, I had no choice but to see myself. To make myself right—to look the way I was supposed to look. The way Liam wanted me to look.

He hasn’t said anything about my weight. But maybe he didn’t have to. Maybe I already knew what he’d say. This is better. This is you at your best.

I swallowed, forcing a small smile as I turned back to Chloe, “Thanks. I just… I don’t know.”

She studied me for a second longer before rolling onto her stomach, propping her chin on her hands. “Well, I hope you’re excited. Big date and all.”

I reached for my lip gloss, swiping a thin layer across my lips, “It’s not a big deal.”

Chloe scoffed. “Liam’s taking you to one of the nicest restaurants in the surrounding tri-town area. You’ve got a really good thing going. That’s a big deal.”

I didn’t respond. Because if I acknowledged it, then I had to define it, and I didn’t know how. It wasn’t normal. It wasn’t easy. It wasn’t even mine. It was his.

All of it.

The knock at the door sent a sharp pulse through me, causing a nervous jolt up my spine. Alex, who had been lying on the floor painting her nails, smirked. “Showtime.”
Chloe glanced toward the door, then back at me with a teasing grin. “Go. Your ridiculously hot boyfriend awaits.”

I grabbed my coat and slipped on my gifted red bottom heels, forcing my fingers to stay steady as I stepped toward the door. The second I opened it, my breath hitched.

Liam stood in the hallway, effortlessly put together—too put together. A crisp white button- down, the sleeves pushed just enough to reveal the veins along his forearms, dark slacks that fit perfectly, his posture relaxed but commanding. The kind of look that wasn’t trying too hard but still felt intentional.

And then there was him. I felt like it was the first time I really saw him: His dark hair that curled slightly and fell effortlessly, the sharp line of his jaw, the hard angles of his cheekbones, navy blue eyes locking onto me with an intensity that made my stomach flip. I knew he was attractive, I always saw him as such. But tonight, I really saw him. It wasn’t fair how handsome he truly was. He didn’t smile right away. Instead, his gaze dragged over me, slow and deliberate, taking in every detail like he was assessing something.

I felt it. The weight of his attention. The way his eyes lingered on the neckline of my dress. The slight, almost imperceptible flare of his nostrils, the way his lips parted just enough to make my skin heat.

Then, finally, his expression shifted—just a little. “Wow. You look beautiful, Caroline.” His voice slid over me like silk, smooth and certain, laced with something quiet but possessive.

I swallowed. “Thank you.”

He stepped closer, just enough that I had to tilt my chin up to meet his eyes. The scent of his cologne wrapped around me, woods and citrus mixed together, and before I could react, he leaned in, his breath warm against my ear.

“See what happens when you listen to me?”

A shiver ran through me, my fingers curling against my palm. It wasn’t a question. And the worst part? I didn’t know if I hated it.

Or if I liked it too much.




