Liam set his glass down with an easy, deliberate movement. “You want to like this—the dinner, the atmosphere, the way it feels to be taken care of.” His smirk lingered, slow and knowing. “But you’re afraid of what that says about you.”

I inhaled sharply, gripping my napkin. “I’m not afraid of anything.” His eyes flickered with amusement. “No?”
I forced myself to meet his gaze, steady, unwavering. “No.”

Liam hummed, unconvinced. “Then answer me this—why do you fight so hard against something that feels good?”

The question settled between us, heavy in its simplicity.

I clenched my jaw. “Because nothing comes without a price.”

Liam’s smirk didn’t fade, but something about it shifted—something darker, something pleased. “Smart girl.”

I swallowed, my throat tight.

He reached for his fork, cutting into his food like we weren’t teetering on the edge of something I didn’t have words for. “You act like I’m asking for something in return.”

I narrowed my eyes. “Aren’t you?”

He shrugged, too casual. “I don’t know. Are you offering?” Heat flared up my spine. “God, you’re insufferable.”

Liam chuckled, low and smooth. “You keep saying that, but here you are.”

I exhaled sharply, taking another sip of water, ignoring the way my pulse spiked under his attention.

His voice dropped slightly, almost thoughtful. “You really don’t like letting people do things for you, do you?”

I frowned, tilting my head. “What makes you say that?”

His smirk softened, but his eyes remained sharp. “Because even now, even with everything I’ve given you, you’re still trying to convince yourself that you don’t need me.”

I stilled, my heart stuttering against my ribs. His words weren’t a question. They were a statement.

My breath stalled, while I willed my voice to stay even. “Maybe I don’t.”

Liam hummed, dragging the tip of his finger along the rim of his glass. “Maybe.” A pause. “But then why are you here?”

The question coiled around my ribs, squeezing too tight.

Carefully, while setting my drink down, “Because you asked me to be.”

His smirk returned, slow and satisfied. “No, sweetheart.” He leaned in slightly, his voice like velvet. “It’s because you want to be here too.”

I tightened my jaw, staring at him.

Because I had a response, I was just no longer convinced it was the truth - And that terrified me more than anything else.

I tapped a finger against my glass, studying Liam as he took another slow sip of his drink.

“Alright.” I tilted my head slightly. “Tell me something about myself that I don’t already know.”

Liam set his glass down, his fingers resting lightly against the stem. He didn’t answer right away. He let the moment stretch, long enough that my stomach tightened with the weight of his attention.

Then—

“You think you want control,” he murmured. “But you don’t.” I drew in air like it might burn.
Liam leaned forward slightly, his voice softer now, more deliberate. “You want freedom. You want peace. But control? You only fight for it because you’re scared of what happens when

you finally let go.”

I swallowed, my pulse stuttering. “That’s not—” I shook my head, exhaling sharply. “That’s not true.”

Liam’s lips curved, slow and knowing. “Isn’t it?”

I clenched my jaw, gripping my napkin beneath the table.

I hated that he said it like it was a certainty. Like he had already decided this about me. Like he wasn’t just observing—he was right.

I forced my voice to stay steady. “And what if I don’t want to let go?”

Liam tilted his head, his gaze sharp, thoughtful. “Then you’ll keep fighting me.”

The air between us felt warmer, heavier, like I had stepped into something too deep without realizing it.

I should have changed the subject. I should have taken the easy way out. Yet instead— “And if I did let go?”
The corner of Liam’s mouth twitched, his fingers dragging lazily over the tablecloth. “Then you’d realize I am always going to catch you.”
A shiver ran down my spine. I exhaled slowly, picking up my drink, trying to steady the way my pulse hammered beneath my skin.

He took another sip of his drink, then set it down with a quiet clink and leaned forward slightly, eyes steady on mine.

“If you could have anything,” he said, low and even, “the perfect life—fifteen years from now. What would it look like?”

I didn’t hesitate.

“Let’s see… I’d be 34?” I said. “I want to be working in policy—maybe international human rights, focus on children, maybe disaster recovery. Ideally with the UN, but if not that, then an NGO with actual reach. I want to manage crisis response, not just watch it happen. I want my name on programs that change things.”

He said nothing.

I kept going.

“I want a family. Two kids, maybe three. I want to come home from a summit or a field office and hear them tell me about their day in three different languages.”

His brow lifted slightly. But he stayed silent.

“And I want a partner,” I added. “Someone who doesn’t flinch when I outgrow the plans we made five years earlier.”

That made him smile—just a little. Like I’d said something that pleased him more than it should have.

“I want to be the kind of woman people listen to. I want to matter. Not because of who I married, or how I look, or what I represent. But because I earned it.”

I reached for my water, letting the silence breathe for a second before I looked up. “What about you?” I asked. “Fifteen years from now?”
He watched me like he was filing each piece of my future away—committing it to memory, planning his strategy around it. Then he leaned back in his chair, fingertips brushing the rim of his glass.

“At thirty-six,” he said. “I’d have a wife.”

There was something in his voice that made me still. It wasn’t casual. It wasn’t bait. It was certain.
“She runs a branch of UNICEF,” he went on, eyes still on mine, “or crisis recovery—or whatever world-saving thing she decides is worth her time that year. The kind of woman who gives speeches in Geneva and calls me out over breakfast.”

My breath hitched—barely. But he saw it.

“We’d have kids. Two, maybe three. Fluent in three languages. Argue politics at the dinner table. All brilliant. All beautiful. All a handful.”

I bit the inside of my cheek to keep from smiling.

“And I’d be preparing for a Senate run,” he continued. “Maybe that year. Maybe the next. But everything would be in motion. The timing. The support. The strategy.”

He said it like he’d already lived it.

“I’d have her with me on stage—not just smiling, not waving politely from the sidelines. Speaking. Holding the room. Making every man in there wish he’d married smarter.”

My face warmed before he even finished the sentence.

He didn’t smirk. Not at first. But there was something in his expression—pride, maybe—that made it harder to look away.

He kept going.

“We’d split time between D.C. and wherever her work pulled us. Summers in Europe. Winters near the coast. A villa on the Italian Coast. Stone walls. Terrible plumbing. Good wine.”

I could see it—just for a second. The light hitting the tile. The echo of children’s laughter down a long hallway. His hand at my waist in some ridiculous diplomatic ballroom.

And then—

“And we’d still fuck like rabbits.” I choked.
“Jesus, Liam—”

He blinked, completely unfazed. “What? It’s a very important part of the plan.” I narrowed my eyes, trying to look annoyed. “You ruin everything.”
But my face was hot, my heart tripping like I was nineteen again, like he hadn’t already seen me fall apart in far less elegant ways.

He just laughed—quiet and pleased.

“No,” he said, his voice dipping low. “I make everything worth it.” And the worst part? I wanted all of it.
He let me sit with it for a moment, let the words sink in before he spoke again, “Your turn.”

I swallowed, forcing my thoughts to realign. “Okay. What’s something about you that would surprise me?”

Liam hummed, dragging his thumb along the rim of his glass. “I like chess.” I blinked, caught off guard. “Chess?”
His lips twitched at my skepticism. “Yes, chess.”

I frowned. “I don’t know why, but I expected something… more dramatic.” “Chess is dramatic.”
I let out a breath of amusement. “How so?”

Liam leaned back in his chair, stretching slightly, like he was settling into something comfortable. “It’s war.”

I arched a brow. “It’s a board game.”

He smirked. “A board game built entirely on power, strategy, and knowing exactly when to sacrifice your pieces.”

I hesitated, my stomach twisting at the way he said it.

Liam tapped his fingers against his glass, watching me. “Everyone focuses on the king, but the king can’t do shit without his queen.”

I swallowed, suddenly too aware of the way he was looking at me. “And you always play to win?” I asked.
Liam’s smirk deepened. “There’s no point in playing otherwise.”

I exhaled, setting my drink down carefully. “Of course you’d say that.”

Liam chuckled, lifting his glass in a lazy toast. “You wouldn’t respect me if I said anything else.”

I shook my head, but the warmth of his gaze, the ease of the moment, was already sinking into my skin.

And I wasn’t sure if I liked it or if I was just losing ground.

I traced the edge of my plate, dragging my fingertip along the smooth ceramic, grounding myself against the pressure building between us.

“Liam, if you wanted me to fall in love with you, why didn’t you just ask me out?”

His smirk was slow, deliberate. He didn’t react immediately, just let the question settle between us, the candlelight casting shadows across his sharp features.

Then, finally—“Because, sweetheart, our love story was never meant to be ordinary.” I exhaled, shaking my head. “That’s not an answer.”
Liam set his fork down, tilting his head slightly. “Isn’t it?” I narrowed my eyes. “No. A normal guy would’ve—” “I’m not a normal guy.”
I paused. No, he wasn’t, and that should have terrified me more than it already did.

Liam leaned back slightly, watching me with a quiet sort of amusement. “I wasn’t going to leave something like this up to chance.” His voice was smooth, assured. “I had to make you mine from the beginning.”

A sharp pulse of something ran through me.

I set my glass down carefully. “That’s insane.”

He hummed, considering. “Is it?”

I forced a breath through my nose. “You couldn’t have known I’d want this.” Liam smiled like the cat who caught the canary. “You didn’t have to.”
I clenched my jaw. My pulse spiking beneath my skin, because he said it with such certainty
— Like he had never even considered another possibility.

Liam leaned in slightly, his voice softer now, more intimate. “I saw you before you ever saw me.”

I scoffed, “What does that mean?”

His gaze swept over me, dark and unreadable. “It means I knew you were mine the moment I laid eyes on you.”

The words hit harder than they should have, because he didn’t say it like he was trying to convince me. He said it like it was a fact.

I exhaled sharply. “You don’t even know me.”

Liam’s smirk softened—not condescending, not cruel. Just confident. “I know you better than anyone.”

I scoffed, shaking my head. “You think you do.” His gaze darkened slightly. “I do.”
I shook my head. “Then tell me.”

Liam leaned back a little, gaze steady but not confrontational—more like he was laying out evidence in a case he’d already won.

“What would convince you that I do know you?” He didn’t wait for me to answer.
“How you’ve got that voice in the back of your head that never shuts up—always scanning for the crack, always finding the flaw, tearing you down before anyone else gets the chance?”

His tone wasn’t cruel. Just true.

“Or how you turn your face toward the sun the second it appears—like your body’s been waiting for permission to feel warm again? Eyes closed, lips parted, like you’re not sure how long you’re allowed to enjoy it.”

My chest went still.

He leaned in slightly, a faint smile curling at the edge of his mouth. “How you’re as eclectic with your news as you are with your music. Deutsche Welle one minute paired Mahler.

Though personally, I think Rammstein goes better with Angela Merkel.” I blinked.
He just watched me.

“How am I doing?” He didn’t pause, “Let’s see, you’re afraid that if you stop moving, if you stop running, you’ll fall apart.” His touch was featherlight, deliberate. “And maybe you will.”

I forced myself to meet his gaze. “And you think you can stop that?”

Liam tilted his head slightly, considering me. Then—“No.” He smiled, slow and certain. “I think I can make it so you never have to run again.”

My chest rose on a delay, unsure if it were safe to breathe. Wasn’t that what I wanted? For it to be easier? For once in my life, wasn’t that exactly what I wanted?

Liam’s fingers traced mine again, slow and deliberate. “You’re tired, sweetheart.” His voice was warm now, coaxing. “I can feel it. You don’t have to fight me forever.”

For a fleeting, terrifying moment— I didn’t want to. I wanted to let go. I wanted to lean into the ease of his certainty, the way he made everything feel inevitable.

Liam tilted his head slightly, his fingers curling gently around my wrist.. “Just let me take care of you.”

I exhaled a breath I didn’t know I had been holding, my pulse hammering, because for once
—just once—I wanted to let him.

That was the most dangerous thing of all.

“How am I supposed to trust you?” I challenged.

Liam’s fingers tightened ever so slightly around my wrist, warm and steady, grounding me here. His eyes never left mine. Not once. Then, just as easily as breathing, he said it.

“Because I love you, Caroline.”

The words hit like a strike of lightning—hot and sharp, searing through my chest. I blinked. “What?”
He didn’t hesitate, didn’t falter, “I love you.”

It wasn’t a confession. It was a statement. A fact. Like he was telling me the sky was blue or the earth was round—like it had already been decided.

I froze , my pulse spiking in my throat. “You don’t know what love is.”

Liam didn’t blink. Didn’t waver. If anything, his grip tightened slightly, fingers pressing into my skin—not hard, not painful. Just certain.

“No, sweetheart.” His voice was impossibly soft, almost affectionate. “The world doesn’t know what love is.”

My stomach twisted, my hand flexing against my lap .

Liam leaned forward, elbows resting on the table, voice low and even. “Love isn’t kind. It isn’t easy. It’s not some delicate thing people get to play with, tossing it away when it no longer suits them.”

I tried to ration my breaths, but he didn’t stop.

“Love is control.” His thumb brushed against my wrist, slow, deliberate. “Love is knowing what’s best for someone, even when they don’t.”

A shiver ran through me. His schedule, his dictations, his leadership -- he saw it all as love.

“Love isn’t about letting go, Caroline.” His eyes darkened, his gaze locking onto mine, unblinking. “It’s about holding on. It’s about never allowing someone to destroy themselves.”

I clenched my jaw, shaking my head. My burn out, my overdose… “That’s not—”

“Love is devotion.” He leaned in slightly, his voice thick with certainty. “It’s protection. It’s knowing what’s right and making sure it happens. It’s about shaping someone into the best version of themselves.”

My breath hitched, a dull ache settling within me. My breath was uneven, lungs that struggled to function as my brain fought to dispute his definition.

“Love is taking care of someone, even when they fight you.” His fingers slid along my wrist , his touch featherlight, still felt like shackles. “Even when they don’t understand yet.”

I swallowed, my voice barely above a whisper. “That’s not love.”

Liam exhaled, shaking his head slightly, almost… disappointed. “Then tell me, Caroline.” His grip didn’t tighten, but it felt like it did. “What is love?”

I opened my mouth, but I had no answer. Not one I could give him, because he had an answer, and I was afraid that if I listened too long, I might start to believe it.

“Love isn’t weakness,” Liam continued, his voice dropping lower. “It’s not distance, or indifference, or letting someone slip through your fingers.”

His grip slid higher, fingers wrapping around my forearm, grounding me in place .

“Love is certainty.” His tone was measured, confident, like he was teaching me something I should have already known. “It’s power. It’s knowing that no matter what happens, no matter how much you try to fight me, you’ll never get away from me.”

A sharp breath left me. “That’s not love, Liam. That’s control.”

His smirk was slow, knowing. “What’s the difference?”

I clenched my jaw, my hands trembling in my lap. “ “Love should feel like safety,” I said, slowly, like I was still trying to believe it. “Like something soft and steady. Something you can rest in, not something you have to survive.”

My voice cracked, but I kept going. “It’s supposed to be chosen. Not forced. Not earned through obedience. Freely given, freely received.” I paused, “Love is a choice.”

Liam tilted his head slightly, his fingers tracing along the inside of my wrist. “No, Caroline.” His voice was like silk, like steel. “That’s what people tell themselves so they can leave when it gets hard.”

That familiar knot cinched behind my navel, swift and tight. “That’s not—”

“Love isn’t a choice.” His gaze locked onto mine, unwavering. “Love is knowing you belong to someone.”

My breath went still.

Liam exhaled, his grip shifting, just enough to feel like a hold. “You’re mine.” His voice was softer now, coaxing. “You’ve always been mine.”

“Liam –” A shiver ran down my spine. I shook my head.

“You can lie to yourself all you want, sweetheart.” His fingers curled slightly, pressing into my skin. “But you know this is real.”

I exhaled as if my lungs burned, my throat tightening. “You can’t just decide—”

“I didn’t decide.” His smirk faded, his expression darkening into something more serious, more absolute. “I knew.”

The air between us felt suffocating, the restaurant falling away until there was only him. Only his hands on me. Only his voice wrapping around my ribs like a vise.
“One day, you’ll see it too.” His fingers trailed down my arm, slow, controlled. “One day, you’ll stop fighting it.”

I swallowed hard, my pulse hammering.

I cut another small bite of food, barely tasting it, then set my fork down and took a breath that felt too big for my lungs. I looked back at him, squaring myself to match him.

“Okay,” I said, steadying my voice. “You have the floor, Senator.” Liam’s gaze lifted slowly, brows rising—not in surprise, but in interest.

“Sell me the future you see for us, that this isn’t just some campaign speech.” I continued. “Sell me the life you’ve already planned without ever asking what I wanted.”

I held his eyes, unwavering now.

“And don’t say it’s because I don’t have a choice.” He didn’t move.
“Make me want this,” I said. “All of it. You want me? Here’s your chance.” A slow smile unfurled at the corners of his mouth—but not the smug, satisfied kind he usually wore. This one was quieter. And because I couldn’t leave well enough alone, “Don’t forget to mention how much quieter I’ll be when I finally fall in line.” He didn’t flinch.

“Alright,” he said, setting his knife down with careful precision. “You want the pitch? Fine.”

He leaned forward, elbows resting on the edge of the table, his voice low and sure, “We build an empire.” I blinked. “You want the career? You’ll have it. Top firms, global reach, published in journals before thirty. Not because I’ll pull strings—because I’ll clear the path. You’ll walk in and own the room.”

His eyes didn’t waver. “You want the city penthouse, the villa in Italy, the summers in Marseilles? Done. We’ll spend August in linen and saltwater, barefoot on ancient tile while our kids run through the garden.”

My chest tightened, but I didn’t speak.

“I’ll build the world around you,” he said, voice quieter now. “Not because you need it—but because you deserve it. Because I’ll never let anything touch you. Not failure. Not mediocrity. Not fear.” A pause. “You’ll still be you. Still brilliant. Still sharp. But untouchable.” His gaze darkened slightly, reverent. “The kind of woman the world envies and never really understands.”

My fingers curled slightly around my napkin.

“You want two kids? Three? None? Name it. I’ll give you a life so complete the past won’t even feel real anymore. Just background noise behind something brighter.” He smiled now, slow and devastating.

“We’ll be unstoppable, Caroline.” Then softer. Deadly certain, “We’ll have everything—
because we’ll have each other.”

He reached for my hand again, and this time I didn’t pull away. His voice dropped lower, almost a whisper, “Just accept it.”

I stared at him, his hand warm over mine, his vision of our future still echoing between my ribs—and I hated how much I wanted to believe it. So I did what I always did when I got too close to falling. I tried to ruin it.

“And when you see a more beautiful, sharper woman in your con law class,” I asked quietly, “or meet a woman with more connections at your internship, or wherever the hell you end up
—when all of this becomes words and nothing more?”

He laughed. Not cruelly. Not mockingly. Just short, surprised. Disbelieving.

“Do you think so little of me,” he asked, still smiling, “or of yourself?” I looked down, but he wasn’t done. “Do men really leave the best woman in their life because someone else walks past them?”

He tilted his head, the amusement in his voice gone now—replaced with something quieter. Clearer.

“Because I don’t,” he said. “And I won’t.” His fingers tightened around mine. “I could have anyone. You know that.”

I swallowed hard.

“But none of them would be you.” He leaned in, voice dropping. “Remember what I said in the chapel all those weeks ago? None of them would challenge me, sharpen me, crawl into my head and stay there like you do. None of them would undo me.”

He searched my face.

“You think I’d trade that for a pedigree and nice pair of legs?” A pause. “No,” he said, softly now. “I don’t want someone new. I want perfection . ” Then—quieter, reverent— “And I already have her, sitting in front of me.”

I was quiet for a moment, turning his words over, letting them settle under my skin. Then I said it—softly, but clearly, “I want two kids.”
Liam’s lips curved, slow and pleased, like he was hearing a victory, “So you’re finally accepting your fate?”

I didn’t smile. “If you can make it worth my while.”

He leaned in, the heat of his presence curling around the edges of the table like gravity itself. “I will,” he said. “You already know I will.”

And for a moment, that tension—the heat, the pull, the future he had drawn for us so vividly
—felt almost bearable.

Almost.

Then I said it, “And stop hurting me.” The words cut the air clean in half. He didn’t flinch. Not visibly. But something shifted.

His expression didn’t harden—but it froze, like the impact had landed somewhere deeper than he expected. Like I’d struck bone. His fingers, still wrapped around mine, stilled completely. No protest. No deflection. Just silence. Because there was nothing else to say.

He knew. He knew exactly what I meant, a nd this time, he couldn’t pretend not to. His fingers went still against mine. No protest. No denial. Just the quiet press of control tightening—not as punishment, but as reverence. His gaze dropped to our hands, then back to my face, as if recalibrating something.

When he spoke, his voice wasn’t soft. It was measured . Intent.

“I know,” he said. Not defensive. Not apologetic. Just… true. “I know I’ve hurt you.”

His thumb brushed over my knuckles—absently, like he wasn’t aware he was doing it. Like touch was the only language he trusted, “I’ve stripped you down. Pushed you. Pressed every raw nerve to the surface. I’ve torn pieces from you that weren’t ready to be torn.”

His voice stayed low, steady. A confessional. Or a vow. “And I’d do it all again.” I flinched, barely. But he saw it. Because of course he did.
He leaned in slightly, not with cruelty—but with the quiet conviction of a man who believed utterly in the path he’d chosen.

“Everything I’ve taken from you,” he said, “was holding you back.” His eyes met mine, unblinking. “And everything I’ve given you is building something greater.”

He paused, letting that settle. Letting it sting if it had to. “You asked me to sell you the future.” A beat. “That was never the hard part.”

His hand curled more firmly around mine. “The hard part was you believing you deserved it.”

The candlelight carved angles into his face, shadows and gold, like a figure etched in stained glass—dangerous, divine.

“You’ll have a name. Power. A life that makes your mother’s silence look like dust.” His voice warmed, reverent now. “You’ll have children who adore you. A career no one can touch. A house that overlooks the water. A seat at any table.”

He leaned in. “And every single person who ever made you feel small will wonder how the hell they missed what I saw from the beginning.” His thumb moved again, slow over my hand. “You’re the sharpest thing I’ve ever touched, Caroline.” Then, quietly—almost like a secret: “And I’ve never wanted anything more.”

I swallowed hard, the air too thick, too still. And then—barely above a whisper—I asked, “And you?”

The question hung between us, weightless and devastating. His gaze didn’t waver. Not for a second.

“I already love you,” he said.

No hesitation. No drama. Just fact.

“I always will.” And he said it like it wasn’t up for debate. Like it had always been true. Like the rest of the world just hadn’t caught up yet.

I didn’t speak at first. Just gave a small nod—barely there. A breath of agreement. Then, quieter still, “Okay.”
I reached for his hand again. This time, not because he asked. Because I wanted to. His fingers curled around mine instantly, like they’d been waiting for permission.

In that small gesture, everything shifted.

I had said okay.

Maybe not to everything. Not out loud. But something in me had tilted. Softened. Surrendered.
It didn’t feel like falling. It felt like relief.

Because I wanted to believe this fantasy he had sold me. I wanted to believe that if I just stopped running, it would be easier, that he would catch me, like he had 10 days earlier. Accepting on my terms was easier than waking up every day pretending I didn’t already orbit around the gravity he’d pulled me into.

He said I’d become something more. Maybe he was right.

Maybe all the breaking was worth it if it built something beautiful.

Maybe this was love. Not the kind I’d dreamed about. Not soft or safe or earned. But absolute.
And maybe… that was enough.


Chapter 24: Taken, Tamed, To Be Determined.





Liam's POV.

She reached for me.

Of her own volition. No coaxing. No command. Just that quiet, deliberate touch—and a nod that signaled acceptance .

And that was it.

That was everything. The fight, the struggle finally bled out.

I closed my hand around hers, steady and sure, letting the moment anchor between us. No applause. No crescendo. Just her fingers curled in mine, and the absolute, unshakable knowledge that I had her.

Not broken. Not forced. Chosen.

She didn’t need to call it love yet. She wasn’t ready for that. I didn’t need the word tonight, just in the end.

The nod was enough. The way her eyes didn’t flinch when I said I love you —that was enough. Because now she was leaning in, not pulling away.

Now she was beginning to believe.

I could feel it in the way her shoulders lowered, the tension unwinding just beneath her skin. In the way she let me look at her without retreating behind suspicion or pride. She was folding toward me.

Exactly as I’d planned. Exactly as I knew she would.

I let the silence stretch for another breath, memorizing the weight of her hand in mine, the stillness in her eyes, the glass between us untouched. Then I sat back slightly, smiling to myself—not smug, not gloating.

Just satisfied.

I had her. And now?
Now I could begin.

Dessert had been delivered fifteen minutes ago. Neither of us had touched it.
The chocolate tart sat between us, half-heartedly melting beneath a delicate scoop of espresso ice cream, ignored entirely until Caroline picked up her fork and finally took a bite.

Her lips parted slightly around the tines, brow furrowed in that quiet, thoughtful way she had when she was trying to decide whether something was good enough to be impressed by.

Then, simply: “Okay. That’s criminal.” I smiled, slow. “Told you.”
She gestured vaguely toward the plate with her fork. “If this is part of the pitch for our future villa in Italy, consider me convinced.”

I arched a brow. “You’re saying I should’ve led with pastry?”

She glanced at me, mouth curving around a bite she was trying not to let me see her enjoy. “Noted for next time.”

I leaned back slightly, my arm draped casually over the armrest of the plush chair, just watching her.

She was lighter now. Not soft—but uncoiled. Like she’d finally stopped bracing for impact. “You’d like Italy,” I said.
“I like dessert,” she said dryly. I smirked. “Noted.”
She rolled her eyes, but the smile tugging at her lips was real this time. Not for show. Not guarded.
I picked up my fork and took a bite from her plate, unbothered by her raised brow. “We’ll have a kitchen with floor-to-ceiling windows,” I said, casually now, like we were
discussing something already scheduled. “You’ll complain about the bees in the garden, and
I’ll pretend I’m not allergic while trying to fix it.”

She snorted under her breath. “You’re allergic to bees?”

“Not the point,” I said, lifting a brow. “The point is, you’ll make coffee in a silk robe and talk about your next move. And I’ll sit across from you thinking, God, she’s mine. ”

She didn’t respond right away.

But her smile faded into something more serious. More fragile. More real.
And then—quietly—she asked, “Will it ever be easy?” I didn’t blink. “No.”
Her eyes searched mine. “But it’ll be ours. ”
She leaned forward, her eyes on mine, fork forgotten beside her plate. Her voice was softer now, but there was no mistaking the edge beneath it. “Never keep secrets from me.”
I tilted my head slightly, watching her. That wasn’t a question. That was a line drawn in sand and salt.

She went on before I could speak. “I’ve heard too many stories of wives being kept in the dark. Shut out of decisions. Lied to. Coddled with silence and called lucky for it.”

Her brow furrowed, the words catching at the edges like she hadn’t meant to say quite that much.

